WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.061
Cross-site scripting is browser-side injection.

00:00:02.061 --> 00:00:06.767
It happens when untrusted content is interpreted as executable script or unsafe

00:00:06.767 --> 00:00:08.728
markup in a browser context.

00:00:08.728 --> 00:00:13.043
JavaScript developers need to think about server-rendered HTML, DOM insertion points,

00:00:13.043 --> 00:00:16.572
frontend framework behavior, HTML attributes, URLs, CSS, JavaScript contexts,

00:00:16.572 --> 00:00:18.534
and JSON embedded in pages.

00:00:18.534 --> 00:00:21.671
The browser is an interpreter with many sub-contexts.

00:00:21.671 --> 00:00:25.201
Output encoding depends on where the data is placed.

00:00:25.201 --> 00:00:30.692
Text inside an HTML element is not the same as data inside an attribute.

00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:34.614
A URL context is different from a JavaScript string context.

00:00:34.614 --> 00:00:37.359
CSS and JSON have their own rules.

00:00:37.359 --> 00:00:41.281
One generic escaping function cannot safely handle every browser location.

00:00:41.281 --> 00:00:45.203
Developers should use framework-supported safe rendering patterns and context-aware encoding.

00:00:45.203 --> 00:00:47.556
Safe DOM APIs help reduce risk.

00:00:47.556 --> 00:00:52.263
Setting text content is different from asking the browser to parse markup.

00:00:52.263 --> 00:00:57.362
Building elements through APIs is generally safer than injecting large strings of HTML.

00:00:57.362 --> 00:00:59.323
When HTML sanitization is necessary,

00:00:59.323 --> 00:01:04.029
it should be done with a well-reviewed library and a clear understanding

00:01:04.029 --> 00:01:05.990
of what the sanitizer allows.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.128
Sanitization is not a substitute for good design.

00:01:09.128 --> 00:01:13.442
Frontend frameworks often provide escaping by default, but escape hatches exist.

00:01:13.442 --> 00:01:17.364
Features that render raw markup, build dynamic URLs, bind styles,

00:01:17.364 --> 00:01:20.109
or integrate third-party widgets deserve extra review.

00:01:20.109 --> 00:01:24.816
Server-provided data should still be treated as untrusted from the browser perspective

00:01:24.816 --> 00:01:27.561
unless the context and encoding are controlled.

00:01:27.561 --> 00:01:32.268
Content Security Policy can provide defense in depth by limiting certain resource

00:01:32.268 --> 00:01:34.229
loading and script execution patterns.

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:38.935
It does not replace output encoding, safe DOM APIs, or server-side controls.

00:01:38.935 --> 00:01:42.073
Client-side validation also does not protect the server.

00:01:42.073 --> 00:01:45.602
Strong browser-side injection prevention combines safe rendering, context-aware encoding,

00:01:45.602 --> 00:01:48.740
review of escape hatches, and layered browser defenses.
